ously we want to  make sure that we can support the um the  800 people who have lost their jobs to  make sure that they can uh get back into  into into good work  but we'll have to work with um  international partners because this is  as you say a complex uh web of  international maritime law um when you  have vessels flagged with other  other countries i i do want to take you  away from your brief again we'll return  to just at the end um nazanin's gary  radcliffe spoke spoke very movingly  yesterday about her years in  incarceration and indeed her complaint  that successive foreign secretaries  could have sorted this out if as we were  told by this trust the other day that  this was a debt that this was an  obligation that we had to the iranians  of 400 million pounds there of their  abouts  if that was a debt that we owed on the  last day of her incarceration it was a  debt that we owed on the first day of  incarceration so i will ask again the  question that she asked why didn't we  pay it before her well first of all it  was a such a pleasure to see nasa  and looking in such good shape as well  i'd seen her after all she's been  through i'd seen richard throughout both  of his hunger strikes and uh you know he  he um he really suffered as did she as  did her family so it's lovely to see her  back but clearly what we had to do uh  the government had to do is to make sure  that um yes it was a debt that that uh  that was there to be paid it was worked  in in parallel but it was a complex  situation to make sure that that debt  worked alongside sanctions uh from a  number of countries around the world  with with iran that was you know it's  not something that was relatively easy  to to sort out but huge but you rewind  you're going to december 2017 when one  boris johnson was foreign secretary and  was trying to secure nazim's release you  know the line from downing street at  that point was there was absolutely no  link between the debt and nasanan's  incarcerati